] [17592186119841 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186476957 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119841 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding the Shortest Distance Between a Point and a Plane Given in Cartesian Form" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119841 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186119655 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119841 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "489887a0-7013-5fb2-8f16-d18616196b0f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120020 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186120724 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120020 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186120725 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120020 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186120726 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120020 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186120727 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120020 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120020 #edb/ref 1045 "The Shortest Distance Between a Plane and a Point" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120020 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186120724 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120020 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049322 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120020 #edb/ref 1050 960.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120020 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "e15d96c3-59f3-5ea3-8c30-d6c68ac605ad" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120250 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186117659 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120250 #edb/ref 1069 "q-33464" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120250 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120250 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the shortest distance between the point $P(5,9,-6)$ and the plane $Π$ with equation $3x+2y+7z=-9.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120250 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6cd2b067-45df-537d-b860-ed07175f92e4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120250 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's start by picking a point on the plane. To do that, we simply substitute some concrete values for $x$ and $y$ into the equation and solve for $z.$ Let's choose $x=2$ and $y=3$ (though it doesn't really matter what values we choose). Solving for $z,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x+2y+7z & =-9 \\\\ 3(2)+2(3)+7z & =-9 \\\\ 7z & =-21 \\\\ z & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we get the point $Q(2,3,-3)$ which lies on the plane.\n\nComputing $\\overset{\\to }{PQ},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =⟨2,3,-3⟩-⟨5,9,-6⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-3,-6,3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also need a normal vector to the plane. For any Cartesian equation of a plane $ax+by+cz=d,$ the normal vector is $n=⟨a,b,c⟩.$ So, for the plane $Π,$ we have $n=⟨3,2,7⟩.$ \n\nFinally, the shortest distance from the point $P$ to the plane $Π$ is the absolute value of the scalar projection of $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ onto the normal vector of the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =|{\\text{comp}}_{n}\\,\\overset{\\to }{PQ}| \\\\  & =\\frac{|\\overset{\\to }{PQ}\\cdot n|}{|n|} \\\\  & =\\frac{|(-3)\\cdot 3+(-6)\\cdot 2+3\\cdot 7|}{\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{7}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|-9-12+21|}{\\sqrt{9+4+49}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|\\,0\\,|}{\\sqrt{62}} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $P$ lies on the plane." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120295 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314481 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120295 #edb/ref 1069 "q-34062" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120295 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120295 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $Q(4,0,-3)$ lies on the plane $Π:r\\cdot ⟨1,-4,5⟩=-11$, find the shortest distance from $P(-2,1,-1)$ to $Π.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120295 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6b172ee6-d9cd-537d-b8f8-03c5ed9b48c1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120295 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =⟨4,0,-3⟩-⟨-2,1,-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨6,-1,-2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the equation of a plane, we get a normal vector $n=⟨1,-4,5⟩.$\n\nFinally, the shortest distance from the point $P$ to the plane $Π$ is the absolute value of the scalar projection of $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ onto the normal vector of the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =|{\\text{comp}}_{n}\\,\\overset{\\to }{PQ}| \\\\  & =\\frac{|\\overset{\\to }{PQ}\\cdot n|}{|n|} \\\\  & =\\frac{|6\\cdot 1+(-1)\\cdot (-4)+(-2)\\cdot 5|}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(-4)^{2}+{5}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|6+4-10|}{\\sqrt{1+16+25}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|\\,0\\,|}{\\sqrt{42}} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $P$ lies on the plane $Π.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120724 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120725 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120724 #edb/ref 1061 17940 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120724 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "9e02b271-c1ce-518a-b549-4e83a2061ea7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120724 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186121054 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120725 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120726 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120725 #edb/ref 1061 17941 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120725 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3779ed6b-358c-5101-8bd7-9de56abc2ade" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120725 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186119839 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120726 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120727 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120726 #edb/ref 1061 17942 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120726 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "213ff9e2-fe7a-528b-b535-4cf580c6aca9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120726 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186119840 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120727 #edb/ref 1061 17943 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120727 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "fdb4920a-a24d-544c-bdef-93ed800bfdd9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120727 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186119841 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121054 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose we want to find the shortest distance between the point $P(3,0,-2)$ and the plane $\\Pi$ that is given by the equation \n\n$$\n(\\mathbf{r}-\\langle 2 , 3 , 1 \\rangle ) \\cdot \\langle 4 , 4 , 2 \\rangle = 0.\n$$\n\n \n\nFirst, note that $\\mathbf{n} = \\langle 4 , 4 , 2 \\rangle$ is a vector that's normal to the plane.\n\n![](images/7b/7b2dfcd87c1f5e4e2c2b1a0b9a7f73899772e33e1020465b2b38d1b2de29a66c.png)\n\nLet's pick a point on the plane, for instance, $Q(2,3,1)$. Then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =⟨2,3,1⟩-⟨3,0,-2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,3,3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe shortest distance $d$ from the point $P$ to the plane $\\Pi$ is the absolute value of the scalar projection of $\\overrightarrow{PQ}$ onto the normal vector of the plane, as shown below.\n\n![](images/26/26e494eef47db4dba3ac36dfa96c7e9a9d191321b22bb82874f3e74bbd7116ef.png)\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =|{\\text{comp}}_{n}\\,\\overset{\\to }{PQ}| \\\\  & =\\frac{|\\overset{\\to }{PQ}\\cdot n|}{|n|} \\\\  & =\\frac{|(-1)\\cdot 4+3\\cdot 4+3\\cdot 2|}{\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+{4}^{2}+{2}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|-4+12+6|}{\\sqrt{16+16+4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|14|}{\\sqrt{36}} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the shortest distance from the point $P$ to the plane $\\Pi$ is $\\dfrac{7}{3}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121054 #edb/ref 1065 3261 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121054 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "224c4104-9097-5a92-9c37-5d0fe22171a6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121054 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186259994 #edb/ref 1080 "3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186259994 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186259994 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0c91748b-039e-5eb9-a37e-a538a11b547b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186260037 #edb/ref 1080 "5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186260037 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186260037 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "129b0757-3ac6-5753-b6e3-478a8023eb52" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186260261 #edb/ref 1080 "1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186260261 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186260261 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "2b8f6350-fb69-5a5b-bb29-e322e7c392a1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186260407 #edb/ref 1080 "2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186260407 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 175921860